## Why This Is Asked
This tests a practical DP pattern: partitioning a string into k+1 substrings with a sum objective and exact k cuts, including reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes with exact cuts
- Substring value computation via prefix powers of 10
- Backtracking to recover split points

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumPartition(s, k){
  const n = s.length;
  const a = s.split('').map(Number);
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  const pow10 = new Array(n+1).fill(1);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){pref[i] = pref[i-1]*10 + a[i-1]; pow10[i] = pow10[i-1]*10;}
  const val = (l,r)=> pref[r]-pref[l-1]*pow10[r-l+1];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const pre = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){ dp[i][0] = val(1,i); }
  for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
      for(let j=0;j<i;j++){
        if(dp[j][t-1]===-Infinity) continue;
        const cur = dp[j][t-1] + val(j+1,i);
        if(cur>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=cur; pre[i][t]=j; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct using n and k
  let cuts=[]; let i=n, t=k; while(t>0){ const j=pre[i][t]; cuts.push(j); i=j; t--; }
  cuts.reverse();
  return {sum: dp[n][k], cuts};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to optimize for very large n (e.g., n up to 1e5)?
- How to handle large sums (big integers) or modulo arithmetic?
- Could you adapt for non-contiguous partitions with penalties?

## Why This Is Asked

This tests a standard DP with an adjacency constraint and a limit on picks, plus reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- DP over i and t with i-2 due to adjacency
- Reconstruction via a take/parent trace
- Space/time trade-offs; potential optimizations

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxNonAdjSum(a, k) {
  const n = a.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      if (t > 0 && i >= 2 && dp[i-2][t-1] + a[i-1] > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-2][t-1] + a[i-1];
        take[i][t] = true;
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruct indices
  const indices = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    if (take[i][t]) {
      indices.push(i-1);
      i -= 2;
      t--;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }
  return { maxProfit: dp[n][k], indices: indices.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Problem Is Asked
This problem combines prefix DP with an inner backward sweep to compute the optimal segment ending at each position. It tests understanding of state decomposition, reconstruction logic, and handles edge cases like negative values naturally.

## Key Concepts
- DP state: dp[i][t] tracks the optimal value using the first i elements with t segments
- Inner loop: expanding the left boundary computes segment maximum on the fly
- Reconstruction: store predecessor pointers to recover exact subarray boundaries
- Complexity: O(n²k) time, O(nk) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueSubarrays(A, k) {
    const n = A.length;
    const dp = Array(n + 1).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
    const prev = Array(n + 1).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
    dp[0][0] = 0;

    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
            let curMax = -Infinity;
            for (let l = i; l >= 1; l--) {
                curMax = Math.max(curMax, A[l - 1]);
                const val = (dp[l - 1][t - 1] !== -Infinity ? dp[l - 1][t - 1] + curMax : -Infinity);
                if (val > dp[i][t]) {
                    dp[i][t] = val;
                    prev[i][t] = l;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    const result = [];
    let i = n, t = k;
    while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
        const l = prev[i][t];
        result.unshift([l, i]);
        i = l - 1;
        t--;
    }
    return { value: dp[n][k], boundaries: result };
}
```

